#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166517 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ed13b87-51ab-547d-a9ed-960eda7db24e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "285e2f4a-e8d0-5f5e-a2d2-fe5ba8476358" :question/math-academy-id "e-8256" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\cos(xy), x=t$, and $y=e^t$, compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3173/Source/Images/e0bfff900fe36a4bdd1a679c435ccf0b.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t$, where $x = x(t)$ and $y=y(t)$, can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} =-y\\sin (xy), \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =-x\\sin (xy)\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} &= -e^{t}\\sin (te^{t}), \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} &= -t\\sin (te^{t})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = 1, \\frac{dy}{dt} = e^{t}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dt} &= \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt} + \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt} \\\\\n&= [- e^{t}\\sin (te^{t})] \\cdot 1 + [- t\\sin (te^{t})] \\cdot e^{t} \\\\\n&=-e^{t}\\sin (te^{t}) - te^{t}\\sin (te^{t}) \\\\\n&= - (1 + t)e^{t}\\sin (te^{t})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166518 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1457941c-efa8-556f-9df7-3c5ae7b44ced" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable in the Special Case"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7da1ada0-43e5-5807-b2c2-2a254a76cf27" :question/math-academy-id "e-8257" :question/problem "If $f(x,y) = 2\\sqrt{x+y}$ and $y=\\cos x$, compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y = y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + y}}, \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + y}}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -\\sin x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}} \\cdot (-\\sin x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}} - \\frac{\\sin x}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - \\sin x}{\\sqrt{x + \\cos x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166519 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1491963b-fbd8-57fa-bdde-f2d6705fbb83" :question/math-academy-id "e-8258" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2$, where $x = s\\cos{t}$ and $y = t\\sin{s}$. Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3173/Source/Images/6bba4306b4e5823056f9eea8e7d48a2a.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t$, where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y = y(s,t)$, can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 2x, \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = 2y\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} &= 2s\\cos t, \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} &= 2t\\sin s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} &= -s\\sin t, \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} &= \\sin s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} &= \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} \\\\\n&= (2s\\cos t)(-s\\sin t) + (2t\\sin s)(\\sin s) \\\\\n&=-2s^{2}\\sin t\\cos t + 2t\\sin^{2} s \\\\\n&= 2t\\sin^{2} s - 2s^{2}\\sin t\\cos t \\\\\n&= 2t\\sin^{2} s - s^{2}\\sin 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the identity\n\n$$\n\\sin 2t = 2\\sin t\\cos t\n$$\n\nin the above simplification."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166520 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c243ca-5d46-58d2-bd16-28075c04d2db" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function of Three Intermediate Variables and Two Independent Variables"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e535fe44-a3cb-5c11-831c-118b90582c0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8259" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z) = e^{xy + z}$, where $x = s + t$, $y = s-t$ and $z = -s^2$. Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3173/Source/Images/6edee879a5f94e8ebd640b97917195c1.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t$, where\n\n$$\nx=x(s,t), y = y(s,t)\n$$\n\nand $z = z(s,t)$, can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x, y$ and $z$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = ye^{xy + z}, \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = xe^{xy + z}, \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z} = e^{xy + z}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} &= (s - t)e^{(s + t)(s - t) - s^{2}} = (s - t)e^{-t^{2}}, \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} &= (s + t)e^{(s + t)(s - t) - s^{2}} = (s + t)e^{-t^{2}}, \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z} &= e^{(s + t)(s - t) - s^{2}} = e^{-t^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$, $y$, and $z$ with respect to $t$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} &= 1, \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} &= -1, \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial t} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} &= (s - t)e^{-t^{2}} \\cdot 1 + (s + t)e^{-t^{2}} \\cdot (-1) + e^{-t^{2}} \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= (s - t - s - t)e^{-t^{2}} \\\\\n&=-2te^{-t^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ac48423-b1a6-5729-9305-5a0b35417b3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "te^{st}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165044} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3b5b84-e8e3-5a19-9667-992ceeaa8a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "se^{2s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165045} {:answer/id #uuid "08a5bbc3-66f9-58d6-978b-b890eafaf65d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "te^{2s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165046} {:answer/id #uuid "78ffae4a-1094-5f42-82ea-fc7cb5423b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ste^{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165047} {:answer/id #uuid "7938068f-a64c-5fa7-959c-06d79d4adefa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "se^{st}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce3b5b84-e8e3-5a19-9667-992ceeaa8a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "se^{2s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165045} :answer-field/id #uuid "03ea9526-0f30-56be-a9c1-49879378e893" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a13d6706-d1e1-54bc-9c0b-137475532529" :question/math-academy-id "q-37205" :question/problem "Let $f(x, y) = 2x + xy$, where $x = se^{s}$ and $y = te^{s}$. Calculate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Keep $s$ fixed. Then $x_t=0$, $y_t=e^s$, $f_x=2+y$, and $f_y=x$. Therefore $$f_t=f_xx_t+f_yy_t=(2+y)0+xe^s=(se^s)e^s=se^{2s}.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ed13b87-51ab-547d-a9ed-960eda7db24e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a642b662-0ded-5132-8065-d99a762d6327" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\csc t - \\tan t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165164} {:answer/id #uuid "d388d99b-09b4-5840-a8a2-abf24ee32617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cot t - \\tan t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165165} {:answer/id #uuid "0fbda1bf-7f23-5db0-98b1-cd15d493c4de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cot t + 2\\csc t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165166} {:answer/id #uuid "0feef7ed-d661-523e-ad86-303520275861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec t - \\tan t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165167} {:answer/id #uuid "55799226-fdf3-56f0-b927-6e5d5f865617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\tan t - \\cot t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d388d99b-09b4-5840-a8a2-abf24ee32617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cot t - \\tan t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165165} :answer-field/id #uuid "14a099a6-c30e-518b-96a4-9fc1c5e6e5e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19a64e51-db17-58fb-852b-340494362842" :question/math-academy-id "q-95078" :question/problem "If $f(x, y) = \\ln (xy^{2}), x = \\cos t$ and $y = \\sin t$, compute $\\frac{df}{dt}$ for $0 < t < \\frac{\\pi }{2}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "On the given interval, $x,y>0$ and $f=\\ln x+2\\ln y$. Hence $f_x=1/x$ and $f_y=2/y$. The chain rule gives $$\\frac{df}{dt}=\\frac{-\\sin t}{\\cos t}+\\frac{2\\cos t}{\\sin t}=2\\cot t-\\tan t.$$"}]}
